            <ArticleTitle>Sonographically detected thickened small bowel in a case of paediatric Salmonella Typhimurium enteritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>54-55</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/j.2205-0140.2014.tb00087.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Introduction: A five-year-old year old girl developed fever of 40.3° C, vomiting, mild general abdominal pain, followed by bloody diarrhea. Description: Ultrasonography revealed a length of symmetrically thickened terminal ileum, more than 20 cm long, with walls greater than 0.5 cm containing a long narrow lumen &lt; 1 mm wide. Conclusion: Given the difficulty of diagnosing the cause of enteritis in young children, ultrasonography represents a simple, non invasive technique which may demonstrate suggestive mural thickening.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
